1. Bug scenario & root cause  
   - In your screenshots you expect the entire quoted URL inside the CSS  
     `url('…')` to be tokenized as one string (alias `url`), but instead only the leading portion (`'https:`) is picked up and the rest (for example the `@300` bit) is lexed as an at-rule.  
   - Underneath, Prism’s CSS grammar defines a rather complex `string` regex and then builds the `url(...)` matcher from it. That original `string` pattern inadvertently fails to swallow the full quoted URL when it contains certain punctuation (in particular the `@`), so Prism falls back to its at-rule matcher mid-string.  
   - The fix is to simplify the `string` pattern to a classic “quote, anything except an unescaped newline or matching quote, then quote” form. This revised pattern will correctly match `'https://…@300;…'` in one go.

2. Location in code  
   - In **components/prism-css.js** the offending line is:
     ```js
     var string = /(?:"(?:\\(?:\r\n|[\s\S])|[^"\\\r\n])*"|'(?:\\(?:\r\n|[\s\S])|[^'\\\r\n])*')/;
     ```
   - In **components/prism-css.min.js** it’s the corresponding `var e=…` definition.

